            <ArticleTitle>Bronchospasm after intravenous lidocaine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1260-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1213/ane.0b013e31817d8637</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>IV lidocaine (1.5 mg/kg) administered to facilitate endotracheal intubation was associated with transient bronchospasm in a 17-month-old-female with mild intermittent asthma. Immediately after lidocaine administration, the patient developed diffuse bilateral expiratory wheezes and dramatic increases in peak inspiratory pressure. Over approximately 5 min the episode resolved and an uneventful anesthetic course followed. This is consistent with recent clinical studies suggesting that IV lidocaine may cause airway narrowing in asthmatics. Practitioners should be aware of this potential complication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
